2|0@zen^1440542195#cuflwiu:mujushingyo$:Where does the phrase "mystical Ki power" appear anywhere in the collection of Tohei's aphorisms you linked to? By the way, it's a good collection; thanks for pointing it out to me. Also, where is there any mention of an egg timer in the final link? I do in fact teach some of these things, and see no contradiction between that and doing Zen, just as Bodhidharma sat rapt before a wall for 9 years and also taught the Shaolin monks the flowing, energetic physical exercises that became the basis of Gong Fu. The stillness of Zen isn't something apart from the energy of life; Original Nature expresses itself in the physical body. Or, as Tohei puts it: &gt;Having no color, no odor and no shape, the mind is not something that can be grasped by the senses. However, based on the principle that the mind and body are actually one, we can know the state of this ungraspable mind by testing the body, which is available to our senses. It seems to me that you are not right now "contemplating and illuminating all dharmas without grasping or rejecting them," as Hui-Neng taught, since you clearly grasp some things as being "Zen" and reject others as being "not Zen." What's more, you make these judgments based not on your own experiences, but on books you've read. Can you explain how this attitude is going to help you to see your nature? 
